  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia Joint Response 2024-2026</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">North Wollo Zone had been experiencing multiple cycles of violence and displacement over the past couple of years due to the escalated war conflict between the Federal Government of Ethiopia and the Tigray People Liberation front. Among the North Wollo Woredas, Gidan was occupied by rebel groups two times which makes the community be-ing conflict affected and vulnerable one. Gidan woreda has 23 kebeles and most of the kebeles are identified as drought and flood prone areas in addition to the conflict. The total population of the woreda is 148,058 (73,183 F) of which 23,450 (8200 F) are under 18, 6252 under 5 children, 8130 PWD.
Regarding the findings of FSL sector, a total of 40,582 (20,724 F) people are in food insecurity. In addition, nearly 99% of the population practice crop production as their livelihood source. And due to the crisis 51,659 (25,534F) people lost crop production which is expected to be harvested from 7,310.18 hectares of crop-covered farmland. An estimation of 19, 5391.23 quintals of different crop type production has been lost due to crop-covered farmland damage (maize, sorghum, wheat, teff, vegetables and barley).
On WASH sector need assessment findings,450 water supply schemes are available (28 are not functional due to different reasons during the time of conflict, 51 are partially functional and the remaining 371 are functional sources). The total estimated catchment population ranges from 8 to 1165 per each supply area. Currently, there are 400 water facilities (1borehole, 445 protected spring and 4 other water schemes). Among the total 68 schools (only 2 are secondary schools), 29 primary and 2 secondary schools have water supply. Only 7 primary schools have sanitation facilities. The remaining 37 primary schools in which most of the woreda students are learning are without water facilities and 59 primary schools with no sanitation facility supply. Furthermore, 8 health posts do not have access to water facilities.The current joint response takes into consideration the high need of the people affected. Hence, to accommodate that a holistic service provision approach is required. To achieve this partner implementing in the same geographical area are encouraged to align their ac-trinities as much as possible. This will contribute towards addressing the beneficiaries in a more harmonized manner.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The overall objective of the Ethiopia Joint Response is to provide complementary multi-sectoral and integrated life-saving humanitarian support to conflict and/or drought-affected people in need - both displaced and non-displaced, in the target locations.</narrative>
  </description>
